Now,
 $$
\begin{align}
\text{number of rows you can build with current chairs}
&={} \left \lfloor \frac{\text{number of current chairs}}{\text{number of chairs in a row}}\right \rfloor \\
&={} \left \lfloor \frac{110}{11}\right \rfloor \\
&={} 10\\
\text{Now this school wants to have as few empty seats as possible. }
\end{align}
$$
Hence: $70$ must remain seated in either existing rows or new rows built. So, for that,
$$
\begin{align}
\text{minimum number of rows to chair occupants}
&={} \left \lceil \frac{\text{number of students}}{\text{number of chairs in a row}}\right \rceil \\
&={} \left \lceil \frac{70}{11}\right \rceil \\
&={} 7\\
\end{align}
$$
Hence,
$$
\begin{align}
\text{number of existing rows lessed by new rows}
&={} \text{number of existing rows} - \text{minimum number of rows}\\
&={} 10-7 \\
&={} 3\\
\text{number of chairs to remove}
&={} 11\times\text{number of existing rows lessed by new rows}
\\
&={} 11\times 3 \\
&={} 33  \\
\end{align}
$$
